\label{thm:MainThm}
Let $\psi:\mathbb{N}\rightarrow \mathbb{R}_{\geqslant 0}$ be a function such that
\[
\sum_{q=1}^\infty \frac{\psi(q)\varphi(q)}{q}=\infty.
\]
Let $\mathcal{A}$ be the set of $\alpha\in[0,1]$ for which the inequality
\eq{
	\label{eq:rational approximations again}
\bigg|\alpha-\frac{a}{q}\bigg| \leqslant \frac{\psi(q)}{q}
}
has infinitely many coprime solutions $a$ and $q$. 
Then $\mathcal{A}$ has Lebesgue measure 1.

The strongest published such result is the recent theorem of Aistleitner, Lachmann, Munsch, Technau and Zafeiropoulos \cite{aistleitner} who showed that 
\eq{\label{extra divergence}
\sum_{q=1}^\infty \frac{\varphi(q)\psi(q)}{q(\log{q})^\varepsilon}=\infty
\qquad\implies\qquad \lambda(\mathcal{A})=1,
}
for any fixed $\varepsilon>0$.

In 2014, Aistleitner \cite{aistleiner} established a companion result to the above one: he showed that if $\sum_{q=1}^\infty\psi(q)\varphi(q)/q$ diverges and $\psi$ is not `too concentrated', in the sense that
\eq{\label{slow divergence}
\sum_{ 2^{2^j}<q\leqslant 2^{2^{j+1}} } \frac{\psi(q)\varphi(q)}{q}=O(1/j)\qquad \text{for all}\quad j\geqslant1 ,
}
then $\lambda(\mathcal{A})=1$.

